# fc-10 complete: scripted end-to-end check of the Video Studio flow

Committed as `c1e6736` on `khamis1992/fc-10` (parent `839e7cc`, the fc-06 merge into
`manus-parity`). Implements fc-10 of the Loam "Video Studio" (FilmCraft + EffectCraft)
integration plan, depending on fc-04 (auto-open), fc-05 (state contract) and fc-06
(export).

## Deliverable

`src/scripts/check-studio-e2e.ts` (new, 686 lines), wired as `npm run check:studio-e2e`.

It is the acceptance check for the whole user flow, in two phases:

| Phase | Runs when | What it proves |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A. Flow | always | The real server, real relay, real `video_studio` tool and real export service against a stand-in editor panel. This is the "unit parts pass always" half of the acceptance test. |
| B. Real dist | `electron/filmcraft/dist` present | The tool can see the vendored editor build. The real wasm editor is driven by the browser panel, so its rendering is covered by the web e2e tests; the phase reports a clear `SKIP` instead of pretending to render. |

What is real in phase A: the real agent loop (`src/agent/loop.ts`) with the real system
prompt and the real `video_studio` tool (`src/tools/videoStudio.ts`), the real command
relay (`src/server/videoStudioRelay.ts`) with its SSE `video_studio_command` frames and
the real result route, the real HTTP server (`src/server/http.ts`) including the task
stream and the `/api/files` listing the UI reads, and the real export service
(`src/server/videoStudioExport.ts`), which resolves the export command from the live
catalog, reads the rendered bytes back through the relay and writes them into the task
workspace.

## Assertions (all PASS on the last run)

1. A video attachment plus an Arabic edit instruction auto-opens the Video Studio panel
   (`loam-video` target, `path=media/clip.mp4`, `import=media/clip.mp4`).
2. The agent read the live timeline (`inspect`) before every mutating command. The relay
   sequence is asserted exactly:
   `importFiles, inspect, commands, inspect, execute, inspect, commands, inspect,
   importFiles, execute, inspect, inspect, commands, execute, readFile` — the export
   returns before the post-read state read, matching `runVideoStudioExport`.
3. A manual edit made in the panel is preserved: the user trims the clip to 14s while the
   agent's first state read is in flight, and the agent's cut at 2s lands on top of it,
   yielding clips `[clip_1 0->2, clip_1_b 2->14]`. A rebuild would have produced a single
   clip ending at 20s and no split at 2s.
4. The tool surfaced the manual-edit notice to the agent (`User edited the timeline
   manually since your last action: ...`), found in the `tool_result` events.
5. The rendered export was saved into the task's files (`exports/رحلة-مقصوصة.mp4`, 4161
   bytes) and is listed by `/api/files?taskId=...` — the route the UI reads — as the UI
   sees it.
6. The agent completed with an evidence-grounded final answer in Arabic, not a repair
   loop.
7. Phase B skipped clearly: `electron/filmcraft/dist/index.html` is not vendored in this
   checkout (`npm run vendor` needs network), and the stand-in panel above proves the
   flow.

## Fixtures and model

The clip is a tiny hex mp4 fixture (`CLIP_BYTES`), so no `ffmpeg` and no fixture file in
the repo. The music is 8 KB of filler. The model is an in-script Ollama-shaped scripted
server emitting newline-JSON `/api/chat` responses (`bench/fakeModel.ts` speaks OpenAI
SSE + `[DONE]`, which `OllamaClient.chatLocal` cannot parse), driven by the number of
`tool`-role messages in the request. No network and no model download.

## Docs

`docs/filmcraft-integration.md` gained a `## User flow (fc-10)` section: the four-step
flow (attach + instruct -> panel opens by itself -> agent drives the live timeline with
manual edits preserved -> export lands in the task's files), the two-phase check table,
and the note that phase A needs no network or model download.

